      "quoted_tweet": {
        "type": "tweet",
        "id": "1948760324384973005",
        "url": "https://x.com/TheStudyofWar/status/1948760324384973005",
        "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/TheStudyofWar/status/1948760324384973005",
        "text": "NEW: Taiwan will hold special legislative recall votes in the coming days and weeks. The ruling Democratic Progressive Party (DPP) — the party President William Lai Ching-te—could take control of the Legislative Yuan (LY) at least temporarily if enough opposition legislators are recalled. (1/3) \n\nKey Takeaways:  \n\nThirty-one seats in the 113-member LY will face recall votes. Twenty-four of the votes are scheduled for Saturday, July 26. The recall elections are in 31 of 39 single-member constituencies represented by the opposition Kuomintang (KMT).   \n\nThe KMT and the minority Taiwan People’s Party (TPP) have used their combined majority to obstruct Taiwanese President William Lai Ching-te’s agenda and pass controversial legislation, including sweeping budget cuts. These cuts have jeopardized the Taiwanese ability to resist PRC aggression. \n\nSuccessful recalls against 12 KMT legislators would grant the DPP a temporary simple majority at least until the special legislative elections are held to replace recalled lawmakers. The DPP would need to flip six seats in the special elections to gain a simple majority in the LY. \n\nA legislative majority—even if temporary—would allow the Lai administration to more easily pursue its agenda, including passing special defense spending packages and other policies to bolster Taiwanese resilience against PRC aggression.",
